00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.150
circuit. And in particular, this these

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.109
can be represented as a sequence of

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:19.910
events or transitions which change the

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.830
system from one state to another. So

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.429
remember in my previous video I covered

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:25.189
the idea that these are circuits with

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:28.710
memory um and that they change on a

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.830
clock edge if we're dealing with

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.750
synchronous circuits and so the next

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.389
state is determined by on on the clock

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:36.790
edge will change

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.630
whatever the current state is and

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.549
whatever the current inputs at. So next

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:41.990
state is a function of the current

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.229
inputs and the current state. That's

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:46.470
basically what we mean by a synchronous

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.029
sequential circuit. Uh three techniques

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:50.869
we're going to be examined here. uh I'm

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:53.270
going to in total in this video I'm just

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.430
going to look at the Moore model but in

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.350
subsequent videos I'll look at the Mealy

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.510
model and then more generally finite

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.029
state machines which kind of combine

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:02.950
both of these using the algorithmic

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.030
state machine design approach. These

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.630
differ in the way in which they generate

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.109
